# Snapshot From Hell Notes

## What I was trying to do
Figure out why Timeshift and Btrfs were acting strange and why the system kept freezing, stalling, or throwing “??? remaining” during snapshot operations. I wanted to understand what was actually happening under the hood.

## What I started with
- A Timeshift snapshot containing 1,428,586 files
- Btrfs subvolumes and metadata trees
- CPU0 spikes during snapshot commits
- GPU resets and compositor freezes
- Kernel logs showing stalls and reclaim events

## What each part does
- **Timeshift** — Walks the entire filesystem and creates snapshots. It doesn’t care how big the tree is.
- **Btrfs** — Stores everything as metadata blocks. More files = more metadata = more pain.
- **CPU0** — Handles most of the filesystem and workqueue scheduling. When metadata storms hit, CPU0 gets pinned.
- **GPU layer (i915/xe)** — Needs stable timing. When CPU0 is overloaded, the GPU starves and resets.
- **Wayland compositor** — Freezes when GPU timing collapses.

## What happened
- The snapshot had over 1.4 million files.
- Timeshift couldn’t even estimate how many were left, so it showed “??? remaining.”
- Btrfs metadata exploded and caused massive stalls.
- CPU0 hit 100% repeatedly during snapshot commits.
- The GPU layer became unstable and triggered resets.
- The compositor froze, stuttered, or crashed.
- This forced me to dig into the kernel, the GPU stack, and the filesystem to understand the chain reaction.

## What confused me
- Why the GPU would crash during a filesystem operation.
- Why CPU0 handled almost all the load.
- Why Timeshift couldn’t show progress.
- How metadata storms affect the scheduler.
- Why the RC kernel behaved differently from stable.

## What I want to try next
- Reduce snapshot size by trimming caches and logs.
- Test snapshots on a tuned kernel.
- Compare behavior between stable and RC kernels.
- Monitor workqueue stalls during snapshot commits.
- Document GPU resets triggered by filesystem pressure.

## Random thoughts
A snapshot with 1.4 million files isn’t a backup — it’s a natural disaster. But digging through the kernel, GPU stack, and Btrfs behavior taught me more about how the system works than any manual ever could.
